# Vercel Web Analytics Installation and Configuration

## Summary
Successfully updated and verified Vercel Web Analytics configuration for the Veltro project.

## What Was Already Configured
The project already had Vercel Web Analytics configured:
- Package `@vercel/analytics` was installed (version 1.3.1)
- Analytics component was properly imported and added to the root layout at `frontend/app/layout.tsx`
- Implementation followed Next.js App Router best practices with `<Analytics />` component in the root layout

## Changes Made

### 1. Updated @vercel/analytics Package
- **File Modified**: `frontend/package.json`
- **Change**: Updated `@vercel/analytics` from version 1.3.1 to ^2.0.1 (latest version)
- **Reason**: Ensured the project uses the latest version with the most recent features and security updates

### 2. Installed Dependencies
- Ran `npm install` to update dependencies and regenerate lock file
- **File Modified**: `frontend/package-lock.json`
- Lock file updated to reflect the new package version

## Verification Steps Completed

### 1. Fetched Latest Documentation
- Retrieved official Vercel Analytics quickstart guide from https://vercel.com/docs/analytics/quickstart
- Verified Next.js App Router implementation matches current best practices
- Confirmed import path `@vercel/analytics/next` is correct for version 2.x

### 2. Build Verification
- Successfully built the project using `npm run build`
- No errors or warnings related to Analytics implementation
- Build output confirmed all routes compiled successfully

### 3. Configuration Validation
- Verified the Analytics component is correctly placed in root layout
- Confirmed proper import statement: `import { Analytics } from '@vercel/analytics/next'`
- Component is placed before closing `</body>` tag as recommended

## Current Implementation
The Analytics component is correctly configured in `frontend/app/layout.tsx`:

```typescript
import { Analytics } from '@vercel/analytics/next'

export default function RootLayout({ children }) {
  return (
    <html lang="en" className={`${outfit.variable} ${_geistMono.variable}`} suppressHydrationWarning>
      <body className="antialiased">
        {children}
        <Analytics />
        <SpeedInsights />
      </body>
    </html>
  )
}
```

## Next Steps for Deployment
To enable analytics on Vercel:
1. Navigate to the Vercel dashboard for this project
2. Go to the Analytics section
3. Click "Enable" to activate Web Analytics
4. Deploy the project to production
5. Verify tracking by checking the Network tab for `/_vercel/insights/view` requests

## Notes
- The project also has `@vercel/speed-insights` configured alongside Analytics
- Both packages work together to provide comprehensive performance and usage metrics
- The configuration follows official Vercel documentation and Next.js 15 best practices
- No breaking changes were introduced in the upgrade from v1.3.1 to v2.0.1 for the current usage pattern
